---
/**
 * The seller's typed work outliving the page it was typed on — whatever took the page away.
 *
 * Every mutating form here is AJAX: the handler calls preventDefault() and posts with fetch.
 * But the forms still carry `method="POST" action="/api/…"`, so if the handler never attached
 * — the whole dashboard is ONE module graph, and a single failed import or a throw during init
 * kills all of them at once — the browser falls back to its own submit. That is a full page
 * navigation to a raw JSON response: everything else the seller had typed on that screen is gone.
 *
 * Three halves, and they answer different accidents:
 *  1. BLOCK — the dashboard's JS died. `defaultPrevented` at document level is an exact test for
 *     "did a handler run": every AJAX handler calls preventDefault() as its first statement, and
 *     this listener sits on document, so it fires after all of them. No flag to set at each call
 *     site, nothing to remember when a new form lands (same reasoning as tab-sync.ts's fetch
 *     observer).
 *  2. KEEP — the page went away with work still in it. Blocking alone only trades a surprise
 *     reload for a deliberate one, so a blocked form is written to localStorage. **And since
 *     2026-08-05 that is no longer the only way a draft gets written: a guarded form is drafted
 *     continuously while it is edited** (debounced, `queueDraft` below). The blocked submit was
 *     never the common accident — a closed tab, a crashed browser and a machine that lost power
 *     are, and none of them run a line of our code on the way out. Nothing is ever sent to the
 *     server without the seller pressing save; the draft is local, and it is an OFFER.
 *  3. RESTORE — the next load offers it back, never applies it on its own: silently overwriting
 *     what the server just rendered would be its own kind of data loss. An offer that matches
 *     what the server rendered is dropped unasked, which is what makes the whole thing quiet in
 *     the normal case — a save landed, so the reload shows the saved values and there is nothing
 *     to put back.
 *
 * **Two things the offer used to get wrong, both reported by the owner on 2026-08-07:**
 *
 *  a. *"אפשר לפספס את זה"* — the bar is inserted at the top of the form it belongs to, and that form
 *     usually sits in a panel the seller is not looking at. Panels are hidden, not destroyed, so an
 *     offer in Settings was announced to nobody while he stood in Products. It is now backed by a
 *     FLOATING notice (`#dash-draft-bar` below), which appears exactly when the bar inside the form
 *     is NOT in front of him. **What it then offers depends on where that form is, not on which bar
 *     is showing** — `refreshNotice` states the rule and the owner's reasoning for it. Same tab: it
 *     IS that bar, word for word, buttons and all. Another tab: it names the section and leads him
 *     there, and the bar he can now see does the asking.
 *
 *  b. *"לערוך משהו ואז השחזור ימחק את מה שהיוזר עשה"* — the offer's payload was frozen at load, so
 *     typing into an offered field and then pressing restore wrote the older value over the newer
 *     one. **Newest wins, field by field**: `touched` records what he has edited since this page
 *     loaded, `restorable` drops those fields from the offer, and an offer left with nothing to put
 *     back removes itself rather than leaving a button that would do nothing.
 *
 *     The same set fixes a hole underneath it. Continuous drafting rewrites the stored draft on
 *     every keystroke, so typing while an offer was open used to EVICT the very work the bar was
 *     still offering — a second crash lost it for good. `save` now writes the UNION: what is on
 *     screen, plus the offered values for fields he has not touched.
 *
 * **The one deliberate imprecision, decided rather than overlooked:** leaving a page with unsaved
 * work raises the browser's own "leave / stay" prompt (scripts/dashboard/unsaved-guard.ts), and a
 * seller who chooses "leave" has decided to drop the edit — yet the draft survives and is offered
 * on the next load. Nothing on the way out can tell that choice apart from a power cut. The two
 * costs are not comparable: an unwanted offer is one dismissible bar, and the alternative is an
 * hour of typing that no action can bring back.
 *
 * MUST stay `is:inline`: its whole job is to survive the module graph failing, which it cannot
 * do from inside that graph. That also means the restore half works on a still-broken load.
 *
 * Continuous drafting is limited to forms the load-time scan actually found (`data-draft-live`,
 * stamped there) — a draft that no later load can locate is quota spent on nothing. That excludes
 * the inline product editor on any page but the first, which products.ts builds on demand.
 *
 * Never stored: file and password inputs, and the CSRF token — see SKIP_NAME below.
 *
 * A form that genuinely wants the browser to post it (server-rendered redirect flows — logout,
 * store creation) opts out with `data-native-submit`.
 */
interface Props {
  /** What the seller is told when a save was blocked — his typed values stay on screen. */
  message: string;
  /** The restore offer on the next load, and its two buttons. */
  draftFound: string;
  draftRestore: string;
  draftDiscard: string;
  /** The floating notice, when the offer is in a tab he is NOT in: it names the section
   *  (`{section}` is the tab's own label, and it is the CONTROL that leads him there — see the
   *  markup) instead of answering for him. */
  draftNotice: string;
  /** Said when the product a waiting draft belongs to is not on the table's page — the draft is
   *  kept and offered again the moment that row comes up. */
  draftOpenFailed: string;
  /** Tacked onto the end of the sentence when more products are waiting than the one it names —
   *  the notice leads him to one, the marks in the table show him the rest. Two forms because
   *  "ובעוד 1 מוצרים" is not Hebrew; `{count}` is the number beyond the named one. */
  draftNoticeMoreOne: string;
  draftNoticeMore: string;
}
const {
  message, draftFound, draftRestore, draftDiscard, draftNotice, draftOpenFailed,
  draftNoticeMoreOne, draftNoticeMore,
} = Astro.props;
---

{/* The offer he would otherwise miss. Server-rendered and hidden rather than injected by the
    script, for the two reasons the dashboard already settled: an injected overlay flashes before
    its styles land, and this component's whole contract is surviving a dead module graph — markup
    that only exists if JS built it cannot do that.

    `!hidden`, not the `hidden` attribute: this is display:flex and a flex utility beats the
    attribute's display:none (the trap is documented across the dashboard).

    **It sits at the BOTTOM** — the common case is this bar alone on a fresh load, and the first
    version's permanent third slot floated it a third of the way up the phone for no visible reason
    (owner, 2026-08-07: "פתאום עלתה למעלה, היא אמורה להיות בתחתית"). It used to step over BOTH
    reserved slots when either of the other two was out, which left an empty bar's worth of air
    whenever only the lower one was filled (owner, סשן א׳ §3). The offsets are now computed from
    what is actually on screen — `scripts/dashboard/bar-stack.ts` — and this markup only carries the
    resting value, which is also the right answer on the dead-module-graph load this component
    exists for, where no other bar can appear.

    Still positions ITSELF rather than sharing a stack container — the elevation guard wants the
    element casting a shadow to declare that it floats, and an ancestor cannot vouch for it. */}
<div
  id="dash-draft-bar"
  role="status"
  aria-live="polite"
  class="!hidden fixed bottom-6 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 z-[9998] flex items-center gap-3 max-w-[calc(100vw-2rem)] py-[0.55rem] ps-[0.9rem] pe-[0.55rem] rounded-[var(--radius-lg)] border [border-color:var(--color-border)] [background:var(--color-surface)] shadow-[var(--shadow-card)] text-[0.85rem]"
>
  <svg class="shrink-0 max-w-none [color:var(--color-warning)]" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
    <path d="M3 3v5h5"/><path d="M3.05 13A9 9 0 1 0 6 5.3L3 8"/><path d="M12 7v5l4 2"/>
  </svg>
  {/* Three server-rendered pieces, filled by `refreshNotice` — so the section's name can be the
      control that leads him there without any script building markup. It replaced a "קח אותי לשם"
      button beside the sentence (owner, סשן א׳ §3): the sentence already names the one place the
      press could mean, and two bars can be out at once, each ending in the same three words. */}
  <span id="dash-draft-msg">
    <span data-notice-pre></span
    ><button
      type="button"
      id="dash-draft-go"
      class="!hidden p-0 bg-transparent border-0 font-[inherit] text-[inherit] cursor-pointer underline underline-offset-[3px] [color:var(--color-text)] transition-colors duration-100 hover:[color:var(--color-primary)]"
    ></button
    ><span data-notice-post></span>
  </span>
  {/* Which of these is out depends on WHERE the offer is, not on which bar is showing — see
      `refreshNotice`. All carry `!hidden` rather than the `hidden` attribute for the usual
      reason: `.btn` is display:inline-flex and beats the attribute's display:none. */}
  <button type="button" id="dash-draft-restore" class="!hidden btn btn--sm shrink-0">{draftRestore}</button>
  <button type="button" id="dash-draft-discard" class="!hidden btn btn--ghost btn--sm shrink-0">{draftDiscard}</button>
</div>

<script is:inline define:vars={{ msg: message, draftFound, draftRestore, draftDiscard, draftNotice, draftOpenFailed, draftNoticeMoreOne, draftNoticeMore }}>
  (function () {
    var PREFIX = 'dz-draft:';
    var MAX_AGE = 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
    // Long enough that a burst of typing is one write, short enough that what a crash takes is a
    // word rather than a paragraph. Every keystroke restarts it; leaving the tab flushes it.
    var DRAFT_DELAY = 700;
    var SKIP = { file: 1, password: 1, submit: 1, button: 1, reset: 1, image: 1 };
    // Never stored, and never restored over the fresh one the server just rendered: a CSRF token
    // (src/lib/csrf.ts) is a credential, not typing worth preserving. A draft written before a
    // sign-in or sign-out carries a token bound to the OTHER identity, and putting it back would
    // turn a recovered form into a 403 the seller cannot explain.
    var SKIP_NAME = { _csrf: 1 };

    /** Which form owns each key, so a second form with the same signature gets the next ordinal
     *  instead of silently sharing the first one's draft. */
    var claimedKeys = {};

    /**
     * Identity that survives a reload AND survives the form changing shape under it.
     *
     * It used to fall back to the form's FIELD NAMES when it had no id, and that is a set which
     * moves: a product's editor grows and loses named inputs as the gallery, the variant rows and
     * the tag editor wire themselves up, and every one of those changes rewrote the identity of the
     * form. Two things broke because of it, both reported (owner, 2026-08-15):
     *
     *   · the floating notice's "שחזר" did nothing at all. It recomputes the key at click time
     *     (`keyFor(o.form)`) while the offer was filed under the key computed during the scan, so
     *     the lookup missed and the handler returned silently — a button that answers nothing;
     *   · the draft the seller typed was SAVED under a third key, written after those initialisers
     *     ran, so the next load's scan looked under the early key and offered the wrong thing or
     *     nothing.
     *
     * So the signature is now made of things that do not move: the form's own id, else what it
     * POSTS to, scoped by the record it edits. Where that is still not unique — two editors on one
     * page posting to the same endpoint for the same record — an ordinal is appended in document
     * order, which is stable within a load and identical on the next one because the markup is.
     *
     * `''` for a form with nothing named in it: it has no draft worth keeping, and every such form
     * would otherwise key to the same string as every other.
     */
    function keyFor(form) {
      if (form.dataset.draftKey !== undefined) return form.dataset.draftKey;
      var named = false;
      for (var i = 0; i < form.elements.length; i++) if (form.elements[i].name) { named = true; break; }
      if (!named) return '';
      var store = (document.getElementById('upload-config') || { dataset: {} }).dataset.storeId || '';
      var record = (form.querySelector('[name="productId"]') || form.querySelector('[name="storeId"]') || {}).value || '';
      var sig = form.id || form.getAttribute('action') || 'form';
      var base = PREFIX + store + ':' + sig + ':' + record;
      var key = base;
      // `isConnected`: a form that has left the document has left its key with it. Without that
      // check every rebuilt editor — the products table replaces its rows on every page change —
      // would take the next ordinal and lose the draft filed under the plain one.
      var taken = function (k) { var o = claimedKeys[k]; return !!o && o !== form && o.isConnected; };
      var n = 2;
      while (taken(key)) { key = base + '#' + n; n++; }
      claimedKeys[key] = form;
      // Cached ON the form, so the answer cannot change later in the page's life however much the
      // form does. Everything downstream — the offer registry, the localStorage record, the
      // notice's buttons — then agrees by construction rather than by luck.
      form.dataset.draftKey = key;
      return key;
    }


    /** Keyed by name + which occurrence of that name, so radio groups and repeated names land
     *  back on the right element even if the page rendered slightly differently. */
    function fieldKey(form, el) {
      var same = form.querySelectorAll('[name="' + (window.CSS && CSS.escape ? CSS.escape(el.name) : el.name) + '"]');
      return el.name + '#' + Array.prototype.indexOf.call(same, el);
    }

    function snapshot(form) {
      var out = {};
      for (var i = 0; i < form.elements.length; i++) {
        var el = form.elements[i];
        if (!el.name || el.disabled || SKIP[el.type] || SKIP_NAME[el.name]) continue;
        out[fieldKey(form, el)] = el.type === 'checkbox' || el.type === 'radio' ? { c: el.checked } : { v: el.value };
      }
      return out;
    }

    /** What the server rendered into each form, by storage key — see `edits` for what it buys. */
    var bases = {};

    /** Offers still on screen and unanswered, by storage key: `{ form, bar, values }`. */
    var offers = {};

    /** Fields the seller has edited SINCE this page loaded, by storage key → field key.
     *  The whole basis of "newest wins": everything in here is younger than the offer, so the
     *  offer must not write over it. */
    var touched = {};

    function save(form) {
      var key = keyFor(form);
      if (!key) return;
      cancel(key);   // this write supersedes any queued one — a blocked submit is not a keystroke
      var values = snapshot(form);
      // An offer still on screen is work he has neither taken back nor thrown away. Writing only
      // what is on screen would delete it — his new typing would evict the very values the bar is
      // still offering, and the next crash would lose them with nothing left to offer. So the write
      // is the UNION: what is on screen, plus the offered value of every field he has not touched.
      var pending = offers[key] && offers[key].values;
      var seen = touched[key] || {};
      if (pending) for (var k in pending) if (!seen[k]) values[k] = pending[k];
      var draft = { at: Date.now(), values: values, base: bases[key] };
      try { localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(draft)); }
      catch (err) { /* private mode / quota — the values are still on screen, which is the point */ }
    }

    /**
     * The fields worth putting back — which is NOT every field in the draft.
     *
     * A dashboard form carries every field it owns, so a draft is a photograph of the whole form,
     * most of which the seller never touched. Restoring it whole would write those untouched fields
     * too, and if a second tab or another device changed one of them meanwhile, the restore silently
     * reverts that change — the exact lost-update `lib/record-rev.ts` exists to prevent, arriving
     * through a door its per-field merge cannot see: the server's own value coming back as if the
     * seller had typed it.
     *
     * So a field qualifies only if BOTH are true:
     *  - it differs from what the page had rendered when the draft was written (`base`) — proof the
     *    seller actually edited it, rather than it merely being in the photograph;
     *  - it differs from what is on screen now — otherwise there is nothing to put back, which is
     *    also what makes the whole mechanism silent after a save that landed.
     *
     * No `base` means the draft predates this (or the form was never scanned): fall back to the old
     * behaviour and offer everything that differs, which is strictly better than offering nothing.
     */
    function edits(draft, form) {
      var current = snapshot(form);
      var out = {};
      var any = false;
      for (var k in draft.values) {
        var want = JSON.stringify(draft.values[k]);
        if (draft.base && JSON.stringify(draft.base[k]) === want) continue;
        if (JSON.stringify(current[k]) === want) continue;
        out[k] = draft.values[k];
        any = true;
      }
      return any ? out : null;
    }

    /**
     * What is left of an offer RIGHT NOW — recomputed at the moment it would be applied, never read
     * from what `edits` decided at load.
     *
     * That freeze was the bug: the offer sat on screen while the seller kept working, and pressing
     * restore wrote its load-time payload over whatever he had typed in the meantime. A field
     * qualifies only if he has not edited it since the page loaded (`touched` — his own typing is
     * always newer than an offer from a previous session) and it still differs from what is on
     * screen (nothing to put back otherwise).
     */
    function restorable(key) {
      var o = offers[key];
      if (!o) return null;
      var current = snapshot(o.form);
      var seen = touched[key] || {};
      var out = {};
      var any = false;
      for (var k in o.values) {
        if (seen[k]) continue;
        if (JSON.stringify(current[k]) === JSON.stringify(o.values[k])) continue;
        out[k] = o.values[k];
        any = true;
      }
      return any ? out : null;
    }

    /** Take the offer off the screen — answered, superseded, or emptied by his own typing. */
    function closeOffer(key) {
      var o = offers[key];
      if (!o) return;
      delete offers[key];
      if (o.bar.parentNode) o.bar.remove();
      refreshNotice();
    }

    function drop(form) {
      var key = keyFor(form);
      if (!key) return;
      cancel(key);
      try { localStorage.removeItem(key); } catch (err) { /* ignore */ }
    }

    /** Pending debounced writes, BY STORAGE KEY rather than one shared timer: two forms edited
     *  within the same window must not cancel each other, and the key is the identity that both
     *  the write and the later restore already agree on. */
    var timers = {};

    function cancel(key) {
      if (timers[key]) { clearTimeout(timers[key]); delete timers[key]; }
    }

    /** An edit landed in a guarded form — draft it, shortly. `data-draft-live` is the load-time
     *  scan's promise that a later load can find this form again; without it the write is quota
     *  spent on a draft nobody will ever be offered. */
    function queueDraft(e) {
      var el = e.target;
      if (!el || !el.closest) return;
      var form = el.closest('form[data-unsaved-guard][data-draft-live]');
      if (!form) return;
      var key = keyFor(form);
      if (!key) return;
      // Remember WHICH field he just touched, before anything else: this is the record that keeps a
      // later restore from writing over it, and it has to be taken even when no offer is open —
      // one can be opened by nothing else, but the draft written below is measured against it.
      if (el.name && !SKIP[el.type] && !SKIP_NAME[el.name]) {
        (touched[key] || (touched[key] = {}))[fieldKey(form, el)] = 1;
        // His own typing may have answered the offer field by field. An offer with nothing left to
        // put back is a button that would do nothing, so it goes rather than sits there lying.
        if (offers[key] && !restorable(key)) closeOffer(key);
      }
      cancel(key);
      timers[key] = setTimeout(function () { delete timers[key]; save(form); }, DRAFT_DELAY);
    }

    /** Returns the FIRST element it actually changed, so the caller can put THAT in front of him
     *  rather than the form. See `reveal`. */
    function apply(form, values) {
      // BEFORE anything moves: what is in these fields right now is what was last saved, and the
      // moment the draft lands on top of it this form holds unsaved work. The unsaved guard takes
      // its baseline on the first focus/pointer press inside the form, which a restore pressed on
      // the FLOATING notice never produces — so it had no baseline, `isDirty` answered false, and
      // walking to another tab said nothing about work that had just been put back (owner,
      // סשן א׳ §1). unsaved-guard.ts#remember owns the other half.
      form.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('dash:willrewritefields', { bubbles: true }));
      var firstChanged = null;
      for (var i = 0; i < form.elements.length; i++) {
        var el = form.elements[i];
        if (!el.name || el.disabled || SKIP[el.type] || SKIP_NAME[el.name]) continue;
        var saved = values[fieldKey(form, el)];
        if (!saved) continue;
        // Compared BEFORE the write: a draft holds every field, not only the edited ones, so most
        // of this loop assigns a value identical to what is already there. Without this test the
        // "first changed" field would always be the form's first field, which is the same wrong
        // answer as scrolling to the form.
        var moved = (el.type === 'checkbox' || el.type === 'radio') ? (el.checked !== !!saved.c)
          : (typeof saved.v === 'string' && el.value !== saved.v);
        if (moved && !firstChanged) firstChanged = el;
        if (el.type === 'checkbox' || el.type === 'radio') el.checked = !!saved.c;
        else if (typeof saved.v === 'string') el.value = saved.v;
        // Everything downstream — the sale preview, the unsaved-changes baseline, the scope
        // toggles — listens for these rather than being poked field by field from here.
        el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
        el.dispatchEvent(new Event('change', { bubbles: true }));
      }
      // A widget that PAINTS from a field cannot learn about it from that field's own events — the
      // image cropper and the category pickers each keep their picture in a hidden input and their
      // chips/preview in the DOM, so a restored hidden value would sit under a preview showing
      // something else, and the next save would write the one the seller cannot see. Same statement
      // "discard changes" makes, so it is the same event (unsaved-guard.ts owns the pair).
      form.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('dash:fieldsrewritten', { bubbles: true }));
      return firstChanged;
    }

    function offer(form, values) {
      var key = keyFor(form);
      var bar = document.createElement('div');
      bar.className = 'flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap mb-3 py-2 px-2.5 rounded-[var(--radius-sm)] border border-[color:var(--color-border)] bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] text-[0.82rem]';
      bar.setAttribute('role', 'status');

      // The same mark its floating twin carries, in the same warning colour. The bar was a line of
      // plain text on a plain surface inside a form full of plain text on the same surface, and it
      // read as part of the form (owner, סשן א׳ §2: *"לא מספיק נראית לעין"*) — an icon is the one
      // thing on that row that is not a sentence, so it is what the eye lands on. It is the CLOCK,
      // not the floppy: this bar offers work back from last time, it does not ask for a save.
      // `innerHTML` on a constant, because an inline script cannot import and createElementNS for
      // four paths would bury the meaning.
      var icon = document.createElement('span');
      icon.className = 'shrink-0 inline-flex [color:var(--color-warning)]';
      icon.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      icon.innerHTML = '<svg class="max-w-none" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 3v5h5"/><path d="M3.05 13A9 9 0 1 0 6 5.3L3 8"/><path d="M12 7v5l4 2"/></svg>';

      var text = document.createElement('span');
      text.className = 'flex-1 min-w-0';
      text.textContent = draftFound;

      var yes = document.createElement('button');
      yes.type = 'button';
      yes.className = 'btn btn--sm';
      yes.textContent = draftRestore;

      var no = document.createElement('button');
      no.type = 'button';
      no.className = 'btn btn--ghost btn--sm';
      no.textContent = draftDiscard;

      yes.addEventListener('click', function () { acceptOffer(key); });
      no.addEventListener('click', function () { rejectOffer(key); });

      bar.appendChild(icon);
      bar.appendChild(text);
      bar.appendChild(yes);
      bar.appendChild(no);
      form.insertBefore(bar, form.firstChild);
      offers[key] = { form: form, bar: bar, values: values };
    }

    /**
     * Say yes to an offer. ONE implementation, because the offer is shown in two places — inside
     * the form and, when that is off screen, on the floating notice — and they are the same
     * decision. An earlier version gave the floating one a "take me there" button instead, on the
     * theory that the decision belongs beside the form; the owner asked why (2026-08-07) and the
     * theory did not survive the question. The bar inside the form shows exactly as much as the
     * floating one does — one sentence — so the trip bought him nothing and cost a second verb.
     *
     * It still ENDS at the form, because a restore is not a save: the recovered values sit in those
     * fields until he presses the form's own save button, so that is where he has to be either way.
     * Restoring into a panel he cannot see and leaving him there would be a change with nothing on
     * screen to show for it.
     *
     * Drop BEFORE applying, not after: `apply` fires an input event per field, which queues a fresh
     * draft of the restored values — and a `drop` running afterwards would cancel exactly that,
     * leaving recovered work unprotected until the next keystroke. `closeOffer` first for the same
     * kind of reason: while the offer is open `save` overlays its values, so a draft written a
     * moment later would carry the pre-restore copy back.
     */
    function acceptOffer(key) {
      var o = offers[key];
      if (!o) return;
      var form = o.form;
      // Was the bar he just pressed in front of him? If it was, everything this changes is already
      // on screen and the page must not move — a press that changes nothing you cannot see moves
      // nothing (AI_INSTRUCTIONS → no-op interactions must be invisible). If it was not — he
      // pressed the floating notice from further down the same tab — the fields he just recovered
      // are somewhere he is not, so put them in front of him.
      var seen = onScreen(o.bar);
      var pending = restorable(key);
      closeOffer(key);
      drop(form);
      var changed = pending ? apply(form, pending) : null;
